s or skin-coloured, with a classic central hyperkeratotic plug and an even shoulder. Removal of the keratotic core will leave a ‘crater’-like appearance to the lesion. Th... See full list on dermnetnz.org Keratoacanthomas are much less common in patients with skin of colour, but the clinical features are the same. See full list on dermnetnz.org The complications of keratoacanthoma include: 1. Scarring 2. Destruction of adjacentstructures 3. Ulceration 4. Bleeding. See full list on dermnetnz.org Keratoacanthoma is diagnosed on the basis of a typical history, the clinical signs and histopathology. The classic keratoacanthoma has a crateriform appearance when viewed histologically at low power. Higher power reveals enlarged atypical keratinocytes with eosinophilic cytoplasm that do not extend beyond the level of the sweat glands. These featu... See full list on dermnetnz.org Keratoacanthomas must be distinguished from well-differentiated SCC. Other differential diagnoses include: 1. Amelanotic melanoma 2. Nodular basal cell carcinoma 3. Common warts 4. Giant molluscum contagiosum 5. Metastatic deposit 6. Nodular prurigo. See full list on dermnetnz.org Most keratoacanthomas are treated surgically. Although they may resolve spontaneously, it is usually prudent to excise them, unless there is clear evidence that regression is in progress. It is usually best to assume a KA-like lesion is an SCC and to manage accordingly in line with local or national guidance, until proven otherwise. In selected cas... See full list on dermnetnz.org Keratoacanthoma is regarded as benign and thus has an excellent prognosisfollowing surgical excision. Lesions that progress and metastasise have probably been SCC, KA-type all along. Patients have an increased incidence of other sun-related skin cancers and should be advised about sun protection and self-examination. See full list on dermnetnz.org Nov 28, 2025 · Keratoacanthoma is a benign epithelial skin tumor that originates from the pilosebaceous unit. It classically presents as a 1–2 cm dome-shaped lesion and commonly affects older adults in sun-exposed areas, such as the face, neck, and dorsum of the extremities. A keratoacanthoma is most often a solitary, pink or flesh-colored dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin plug developing on the sun-exposed skin of elderly persons. Sep 19, 2025 · Keratoacanthoma (KA) is a cutaneous tumor that most commonly presents as a dome-shaped nodule with a central keratin-filled crater (picture 1A-E) [1,2]. KA most frequently develops on hair-bearing, sun-exposed skin. Keratoacanthoma is a rapidly growing, dome-shaped skin lesion with a central keratin plug, typically arising in sun-damaged skin of older adults. While often benign and self-limiting, it is usually managed with surgical excision due to its resemblance to squamous cell carcinoma. Keratoacanthomas are rapidly growing, dome-shaped skin tumors with a central keratin-filled crater. They most commonly arise on sun-exposed areas in older adults (1)."}, {"title": "Keratoacanthoma in Older Adults | Springer Nature Link", "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-53888-9_66-1", "snippet": "Nov 28, 2025 · Keratoacanthoma is a benign epithelial skin tumor that originates from the pilosebaceous unit. It classically presents as a 1–2 cm dome-shaped lesion and commonly affects older adults in sun-exposed areas, such as the face, neck, and dorsum of the extremities."}, {"title": "Keratoacanthoma: Symptoms, Causes & Treatment - Cleveland Clinic", "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/keratoacanthoma", "snippet": "Nov 18, 2025 · A keratoacanthoma is a fast-growing, dome-shaped bump that often has a scab-like plug in the center. It usually appears on skin that gets a lot of sun, like your face, neck, arms or hands. It’s pronounced “ker-a-to-ac-an-tho-ma.” This tumor is usually noncancerous and often goes away on its own."}]}
